{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening","id":1001603,"title":"Are there characteristics that increase the likelihood of Awakening?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-10T20:06:13-08:00","id":1001603,"title":"Are there characteristics that increase the likelihood of Awakening?","content":"Earlier I made an ill-advised post about why I house-rule a minimum of 3 Intelligence when I run Mage. There was strong and pretty unanimous disagreement there, which is fine. It made me wonder, though--do people here think the only thing that distinguishes the Awakened from Sleepers is Awakening itself? Is it completely random?<br /> <br /> I've always seen mages as being exceptional even before Awakening. Like nascent Exalted but to a lesser degree. For one thing, the starting Willpower for a mage is 5 at minimum, which if I remember right is above the 90th percentile for humans. So there's at least one attribute that helps stir Avatars to Awaken or attracts Avatars that are likely to Awaken. Backgrounds aren't really a set trait, but the fact that mages have higher starting Backgrounds than any other splat suggests that mages have a breadth of experience above what is normal. And of course, like every other WoD splat, they have higher-than-average Attributes though they can be distributed as diversely as anyone's.<br /> <br /> The mechanical trait differences end there. There are no Nature requirements either--but is it equally likely that a Follower or Conformist would be a Mage as a Rebel or Architect? That seems implausible to me, I'd expect certain types of people to be more prone to rejecting Consensus reality than others. The fantasy trope of the generic protagonist who's awesome by sheer luck doesn't seem to work very well in Mage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 11-10-2016, 11:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T20:22:47-08:00","id":1001605,"title":"","content":"At least personally, I view it as less a talent and more a conviction. Believing so firmly in ones personal view of the world that the world actually bends. This requires high willpower. And is also self evident in the fact that Mage has no morality bar. A mage can never be convinced they are in the wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T22:00:46-08:00","id":1001628,"title":"","content":"Will is the primary attribute needed to Awaken. Regardless of potential, skill, aptitude, or personality, all mages consciously or unconsciously are forcing their will on reality. Without sufficient will, nothing other combinations of traits can let someone access the truth of the Tapestry to reach out and rewrite it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T22:53:17-08:00","id":1001634,"title":"","content":"No, a Willpower of 5 is in the top 47% (Werewolf Revised, p. 192), so it is near average. I would think that being a Sorcerer would make it more likely, perhaps a tenfold increase, but I think that former Sorcerers are probably only 10% of the Awakened. No, I think that you have to be extremely insane, on some fundamental level that is undetectable in normal life, which allows you to accept True Magick.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T00:43:29-08:00","id":1001660,"title":"","content":"I think it depends on the tradition.<br /> If you're really intuitive and &quot;in touch&quot; with your surroundings, then you'd probably get on well with the Dreamspeaker paradigm.<br /> If you are diligent and studious and doggedly determined to learn, then the paradigms of the Order of Hermes will be more our ticket.<br /> If you are someone who craves balance in your life, not just in what you do but in how you think and live, then the Akashic lifestyle will appeal much more greatly.<br /> If you long for a world of endless scientific possibility and have a keen interest in outdated scientific methods, the Etherite lifestyle will sweep you away.<br /> <br /> From this point, awakening will be more a question of how able you are to accept the truths presented to you, whether that be through training, or through trauma.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T01:17:58-08:00","id":1001669,"title":"","content":"It's not <i>completely</i> random, but it is sufficiently complex and multi-faceted and personal that it seems like it is. I'd suspect that the only absolute requirement for Awakening is a kernel of belief in the <i>extraordinary</i>. For most mages this might be the supernatural while nascent technocrats might believe in the potential for some sort of extraordinary science or technology. Someone who top to bottom, inside and out just has no room for those kind of things in their lives would never awaken.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T02:39:01-08:00","id":1001691,"title":"","content":"Well... I suspect that all the different factions have different traits that they would assert is key and attempt to engender in any students in an attempt to improve their student's chances. I personally like a theory I'd seen around that no one actually knows for sure, but that the one thing that does seem to help is the personal guidance of another of the Awakened; that goes a long way towards explaining how such a rare event as an awakening can still develop along Master-Apprentice lines for more than a generation or two (let alone the near unbroken lines that most of the factions rely on).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>---------------------------------------------<br /> Supporter of Spirits getting full playable splat design come Exalted 3E.<br /> Refugee from the 3rd Great Edition Wars of D&amp;D. <br /> oWoD Latecomer, Exalted Optimist, Scion ST, Trinity Curious","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/955-yue-ryong","name":"Yue Ryong","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar955_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"147"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T06:31:16-08:00","id":1001734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yue Ryong</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening?p=1001691#post1001691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well... I suspect that all the different factions have different traits that they would assert is key and attempt to engender in any students in an attempt to improve their student's chances. I personally like a theory I'd seen around that no one actually knows for sure, but that the one thing that does seem to help is the personal guidance of another of the Awakened; that goes a long way towards explaining how such a rare event as an awakening can still develop along Master-Apprentice lines for more than a generation or two (let alone the near unbroken lines that most of the factions rely on).</div> </div> </div> </div>It's worth remembering that Sorcery is a thing that exists. Many of those unbroken Master-Apprentice lines (and most of the magical Family lines, like the ones that heavily populate the Verbana) train Sorcerers whenever true Awakened are scarce. You don't really need to teach Spheres to preserve one's magical Tradition, just the Focus that channels them. And a Focus/Paradigm works just as well for learning Paths as it does for Spheres.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T06:56:17-08:00","id":1001741,"title":"","content":"I'd certainly not dispute that, and I do think that Sorcery* should be more emphasised in the setting for exactly that reason, but in spite of Revised doing a lot to bring them into alignment, M20 seems to have deliberately pushed in another direction.<br /> Now, I can see the worry that the presence of Sorcerers as a big part of the core setting erodes the significance of the Mages, establishing people who can (on the surface, at least) do many of the things that the Enlightened can... but I think that the demarkations along the spectrum to true Enlightened Magick helps to reemphasise that Mages are still ultimately human.<br /> <br /> ...My apologies, I seem to have tugged us away into a tangent from the original subject.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\">*or something like it; I often use low-Arete Mages with the Sleepwalker Flaw to approach the issue from the other side.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>---------------------------------------------<br /> Supporter of Spirits getting full playable splat design come Exalted 3E.<br /> Refugee from the 3rd Great Edition Wars of D&amp;D. <br /> oWoD Latecomer, Exalted Optimist, Scion ST, Trinity Curious","edited":{"userid":955,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/955-yue-ryong","user":"Yue Ryong","date":".  Formatting 11-11-2016, 10:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/955-yue-ryong","name":"Yue Ryong","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar955_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"147"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T12:56:31-08:00","id":1001890,"title":"","content":"To re-orient the conversation a bit:<br /> <br /> There's something worth thinking about the history and metaphysics of the setting that were crafted to create the current &quot;now&quot; of the setting. Who Awakens and how they Awaken is something that's supposed to have changed radically over the centuries. In a setting where belief and reality are codependent reality shifted in response to belief; as mages came to believe that the old ways were being lost and the continuity of their traditions were under threat, their fears became manifest.<br /> <br /> To give that some context, in the Mythic Ages, mages awakened in a seemless fashion as the Psychopomps knew exactly the right avatar to link to the right individual such that when prompted by their cultural beliefs in how one achieves enlightenment occurred, the mage's perceptions of the process were one of a contiguous process. Early mages didn't realize there were such things as &quot;avatars&quot; because the fusion of human and avatar was so perfect it never felt like an external addition to their being once it gain power along with them. For untold times, teachers instructed those with potential until their power awakened, and it seemed as natural as any other craft or endeavor for this to be the case.<br /> <br /> Then things changed. For whatever reasons the avatar-human matching process began to crack. Mages began to be aware of having an avatar as a separate entity because their avatars were no longer indistinguishable from themselves. The Order of Reason had something to sink their teeth into besides being splinter Hermetics; evidence that mages were playing with forces far more malleable than previous generations really thought, and reality itself was the playing field mages were actually fighting on. As the OoR's success in cracking open reality bore fruit in their war with the Traditions, it also meant that the why of who awakens changed as well. The growing mismatch of avatar to person, and the formation of Reality Zones, created the space for more and more unpredictable and seemingly random awakenings. The old studious methods still worked in their way, but it became more and more clear as the Enlightment era kicked in that it wasn't how things worked any more and was just one path among many.<br /> <br /> And change continued. Spontaneous awakening from personal epiphany grew to be the norm, with experienced mages fighting to find and recruit over bolstering their numbers by training. The dichotomy of avatar and mind grew larger and increasingly frayed perceptions of nascent mages to force epiphanies away from the tutelage of others to generate such events. The new generations of mages bucked against the ways of the older generations because the world had literally changed and the rules of reality they had to deal with were not bound by ancient wisdom's teachings. The continuity of mentor to student frayed as it simply became less realistic and few of the older generations were capable of adapting to stay relevant (though a new middle generation born to this new world are beginning to be the new teachers with that test in front of them).<br /> <br /> Which leaves mages where the game starts. Anyone might have the spark of awakening in them. Individuality has taken over from tradition to constantly refuse predictability. Only the must basic fundamental metaphysical truths dictate who can awakening in a near tautological way.<br /> <br /> And of course, stepping back out of the game, this is completely on purpose. The game wants mages to be anybody from every walk of life. And most of what I said is later additions and retcons the game as offered to explain how things got the desire point of the original premise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-12T18:07:31-08:00","id":1002410,"title":"","content":"Definitely have to parse this one as each has a distinctly separate-if-still-related bias of thought to it...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening#post1001603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Earlier I made an ill-advised post about why I house-rule a minimum of 3 Intelligence when I run Mage. There was strong and pretty unanimous disagreement there, which is fine. It made me wonder, though--do people here think the only thing that distinguishes the Awakened from Sleepers is Awakening itself? Is it completely random?</div> </div> </div> </div>Is it random (inherently)? No. <i>Can</i> it be random? Absolutely. Some Awakenings are the proverbial definition of random -- a causal-but-not-preconceived event happens which leads to the Awakening of a mortal's Avatar/Fount/Eidelon/Sahu/part-of-them-that-is-god/what-have-you. No rhyme, no reason, nothing &quot;special&quot; about the event/mortal in question (save, of course, for them now having the (A)wakened capacity to work magic(k). For others, on the opposite end of the spectrum, there is a conscious pursuit of mystical (A)wakening/understanding, where in the pursuit of &quot;magic(k)&quot;, proper, or the enlightenment of the self/soul which as a byproduct stirs the mortal's capacity for mystical power/awareness. And for a number of others it can be for a variety of facets/reasons: they happen to have as part of their spiritual make-up a powerful god-shard (Avatar:4+) which has a degree of bias towards Awakening due to its innate nature, memories/mystical workings from the past, being proximal to other god-shards that resonate strongly with it, possessing a powerful (D)estiny or being meta-consciously aware of its own previous incarnations, etc and et al; they have no innate pre-bias towards (A)wakening, but happen to -- via family, friends/acquaintances, or similar -- be raised/keep company with other supernatural beings and consequently find their (A)wakening somewhat more likely to occur than if they had lived a more mundane life; and lastly (though rarely), some mages (A)waken because of the direct mystical workings of others, with the intent of their (A)wakening being the goal (in some cases because such is sought in the specific individual in question or alternately/more abstractly as a blanket pattern attempting to suss out an (A)wakening *somewhere* in *somebody*, and then indoctrinated (or at least guided/swayed) by those who sought out such (A)wakenings caused by &quot;x&quot; efforts on their parts).<br /> <br /> Some/Many of those things have much to do with innate drive/(I)ntelligence, some do not. Seeing, however, (M)ages as inherently having Int:3+ is itself a matter of paradigm/bias-of-perception, which relative to an in-narrative source is perfectly fine (it often occurred that many Hermetics would reject less intelligent/savvy pupils -- and all-but-disregard out of hand the &quot;savage&quot; practitioners, such as would be identified as Dreamspeakers/Spirit Talkers, Verbena/&quot;The Old Faith&quot;, and similar -- who didn't meet their &quot;academic&quot; level of intellectualization/understanding... this, of course, was hubris on their parts, and a number of DS/ST/V/ToF, et al, mages were every bit as (A)wakened as they were, if not as intellectually gifted (might only have Int:1-2, reflecting less of a properly intellectual understanding of things)), but on a meta-mechanic level is an indication of not grasping the inherent diversity of what, exactly, The Awakening can mean, as well as the myriad ways it can manifest with or without any regard to the mortal through whom such (A)wakening manifests, (Again, see Nan from AHS: Coven, as she -- though there are other examples in various fictions -- is a very good example of someone who likely &quot;only&quot; has an Int:2 (possibly one, given that her Per might be rather high, and her Wits -- though she could be surprised/flat-footed -- might have been a 3 if she had survived to blossom into her full (A)wakening.)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200b</span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening#post1001603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always seen mages as being exceptional even before Awakening.</div> </div> </div> </div>And for some, this is true. For a number of others, however, nothing could be further from the truth. Plenty of indicated or sample characters from the various editions were not that exceptional (or exceptional, at all) in their unawakened lives before (A)wakening. A great number *were* exceptional and/or &quot;ambitious pursuers/already of considerable talents or abilities), etc., but not in any way that creates a filibuster-proof majority, by any means. Again, a level of personal bias/preference may be clouding the situation... (That isn't to say that &quot;x&quot; being true for *your* setting is &quot;wrong&quot; -- not at all -- but rather that the nature of the statement -- and the question(s) posed relative to this and the other thread -- show a level of misperception/miscognitization going on that may not be noted by you...)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200b</span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening#post1001603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like nascent Exalted but to a lesser degree. For one thing, the starting Willpower for a mage is 5 at minimum, which if I remember right is above the 90th percentile for humans. So there's at least one attribute that helps stir Avatars to Awaken or attracts Avatars that are likely to Awaken. Backgrounds aren't really a set trait, but the fact that mages have higher starting Backgrounds than any other splat suggests that mages have a breadth of experience above what is normal. And of course, like every other WoD splat, they have higher-than-average Attributes though they can be distributed as diversely as anyone's.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, as far as the Backgrounds go, I can totally help you with that one: back in the Long Ago, in the Before Time (the old, very original forum, pre-2004 -- and, more specifically, at a couple points between 1998 and 2003), The Powers That Be (at that time) commented on the extra points for Backgrounds -- they were to give a little room for adjudication relative to the Avatar Background. One could not put anything in it, if one so chose (because some mages have only the &quot;spark&quot; within them, rather than even a single-steady candle flame), but the inherent mechanics involved were more tied to the game structure than, say, with Generation relative to vampires. So a couple extra points were added to potentially flesh out their mystical background and/or capacities. It went hand-in-hand with how Mage characters have double the number of starting dots compared to the two other major splats (vampires/werewolves, who started normally with 3 dots in &quot;Shit I Can Do&quot;), balancing how most all other splats have a range of inherent powers (healing, shape-changing, superhuman stats, etc.) that mages don't innately have or start with. The extra Sphere dots and points in Backgrounds were just that: a game-mechanic balancer, with no other inherent meaning beyond that. (It's similar to how PC mortals, in older editions, start with 21 Freebie points vs. the standard 15, so has to give them a little more flexibility of (b)ackground that their other, inferior dot-spreads and lack of supernatural ability handicapped them with.)<br /> <br /> And the same thing goes with Attributes, Abilities and similar: they start with the above-normal-mortal spread because (as is assumed with most other splats) the mage has been mage-ing for a while now and has previous experiences doing x, y and z. There's nothing else inherently meaningful behind that other than the &quot;WoD &quot;First Level Characters&quot; have generally more ability and background that DnD &quot;First Level Characters&quot; &quot; relative comparison. (Note how when using the Equal For All option for Sorcerers and other &quot;mortal&quot; characters *everybody* starts with the &quot;standard&quot; spread of points relative to what they are: ordinary mortals get the 7/5/3, 13/9/5 and 15 Freebies spread, with certain tweaks for magic (6 points for Sorcerers vs. more limited points for other &quot;mortal&quot; characters -- gypsies, and similar -- who might have other inherently mystical abilities/perks/what-have-you relative to Joe Public) and similar.<br /> <br /> Your view/suggestion can be perfectly applicable for any given Mage character, or even a number of them; the rationale, however ,falls short when compared relative to the standards of most other splats.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200b</span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening#post1001603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The mechanical trait differences end there. There are no Nature requirements either--but is it equally likely that a Follower or Conformist would be a Mage as a Rebel or Architect? That seems implausible to me, I'd expect certain types of people to be more prone to rejecting Consensus reality than others. The fantasy trope of the generic protagonist who's awesome by sheer luck doesn't seem to work very well in Mage.</div> </div> </div> </div>And again, for *some*/*many* characters, that would be the case: innate talent, hard work/determination, and a shitload of research (whatever that means relative to a given character's history and understanding) is key to their understanding and ability. For a considerable number of others, however, their innate talents may simply blossom (para)naturally via their innate souls (Avatars) Awakening(tm) and they in turn manifesting the capacity for True Magick(tm). No studying. No pre-existing bias towards mysticism (or, in rarer cases, meta-science). No active pursuit of magic(k) in any way, shape or form. *Remember*: a great number of members of many of the Traditions came to be so not because of the tutelage that they, the Traditions, offered, or were pursued to via the character's seeking *them* out, but because *they* discovered a given &quot;mage&quot; (who may or may not have identified themselves by that or similar terms) and swayed/indoctrinated her into their fold/cause. Many house-witches, street-shamans and road-warriors (A)waken to their magic(k) without any predisposition (that is, as understood within a mundane context; as mentioned further up, their inherent spiritual make-up may have had plans since millennia before they were born... but that's a separate concept...) whatsoever. Even the mechanical conceit of the Willpower:5+ rating (the average person having a WP in the 2-3/4 range, per several books indicating so) doesn't necessarily reflect any active drive on the character's part (though it can ,of course, for any *given* character in particular), but rather is another relative concept reflecting how the character likely had to have some above-average sense of determination and/or self and/or mental focus and/or &quot;I'm not going to lose my mind to this crazy shit&quot; about them so that (credibly speaking) they didn't just go insane out-the-gate when they Awakened(tm), in whatever manner, and discovered that the world isn't quite what they thought it was.<br /> <br /> None of that has to involve the the fantasy trope of the generic protagonist who awesome by sheer luck -- there are a great number of other concepts that exist far outside what you are suggesting... though, relative to Mage, there *are* some of the above lucky devils/Harry Potters/the sisters from Charmed/what-have-you, wherein they are awesome because they have high Avatar/Destiny ratings (Harry and the sisters), and have an essential shit-ton of helpful backgrounds out-the-gate (such as with the Charmed girls, who started with a Chantry, Sanctum, Node (one of the strongest in the mundane world for their mythology), Library/Wonder (their super-spellbook), and helpful spirits of their preceding witch ancestors, along with the Lucky and/or Charmed Existence merits... the latter, of course, humously appropriate; Harry had... well... yeah -- he had *a lot* going for him, actually, even if it doesn't list the same, exactly, as with the other example). In those cases, the &quot;XP spent&quot; wasn't always so much about gaining raw power/strength (though in some instances it was), but more so coming to understand *how* to control or utilize the considerable strength/range of powers they had from their initial &quot;awakening&quot; (the sisters had a shitload of starting dots, magically-speaking). That trope, while I wholly agree is over-used by many people and often used as a mentally lazy &quot;just 'cus&quot; excuse for some players (and not a few STs...) relative to their characters, isn't invalid on-its-face as being legitimate relative to Mage's setting -- it *is* just as legitimate there as the one you are suggesting, and especially so compared to the &quot;linear, flat-earth&quot; settings such as in DnD (not bad-mouthing DnD, by any means, but simply cross-citing its metaphysics because they are &quot;flat earth&quot;, and not the deeper, more flexible understanding of Reality(tm) that Mage(tm) deals with, in general).<br /> <br /> As far as Natures... there can be a sizeable number of Conformist mages, ones who were indoctrinated into a given mystical order (the CC, OoH, and certain other groups are more likely than others to fit this bill) and express their Enlightened Will(tm) by supporting the belief system in question. (They can be Conformists, Supporters, Architects (which, in-and-of-itself, doesn't necessary mean anything that causes the mage to &quot;press against the world&quot;), Traditionalists, and even the likes of Child, Loner, CareGiver and similar-such, can be a mage's inherent Nature, and said character in no way &quot;challenge&quot; Reality(tm), as such, but simply support their own beliefs in ways that can be subtle.)<br /> <br /> All of these are very important distinctions....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":".  Cut/paste error, spacing as usual... 11-12-2016, 09:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T17:45:32-08:00","id":1022552,"title":"","content":"i am not sure if the extra attributes are just a mechanic though. i could be wrong but IIRC initates to the arts implied they weren't?<br /> <br /> same for other gamelines for instance vampire the dark ages core rulebook explicitly says that the extra dots are granted by vampirism itsself. so perhaps it's similar for mages?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T17:58:23-08:00","id":1022555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening?p=1022552#post1022552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i am not sure if the extra attributes are just a mechanic though. i could be wrong but IIRC initates to the arts implied they weren't?<br /> <br /> same for other gamelines for instance vampire the dark ages core rulebook explicitly says that the extra dots are granted by vampirism itsself. so perhaps it's similar for mages?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Can confirm. According to Sorcerer Revised pp. 44-45, Sorcerers get 6/4/3 for attributes and 11/7/4 for abilities at character creation specifically because of their unawakened status. This is instead of the Awakened Mage's 7/5/3 and 13/9/5 respectively.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T18:43:56-08:00","id":1022574,"title":"","content":"It is a grafted explanation, and a irregular one. The game loosely used less numbers for less monstrous characters far before any author made an actual effort in explaining why. It simply seemed right for them at the time.<br /> <br /> That is, the distinction on starting dots was always there since the beginning, but was rarely given any true explanation on what they mean. It &quot;just is&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T14:18:11-08:00","id":1023423,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1001603-are-there-characteristics-that-increase-the-likelihood-of-awakening?p=1001890#post1001890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To re-orient the conversation a bit:<br /> <br /> There's something worth thinking about the history and metaphysics of the setting that were crafted to create the current &quot;now&quot; of the setting. Who Awakens and how they Awaken is something that's supposed to have changed radically over the centuries. In a setting where belief and reality are codependent reality shifted in response to belief; as mages came to believe that the old ways were being lost and the continuity of their traditions were under threat, their fears became manifest.<br /> <br /> To give that some context, in the Mythic Ages, mages awakened in a seemless fashion as the Psychopomps knew exactly the right avatar to link to the right individual such that when prompted by their cultural beliefs in how one achieves enlightenment occurred, the mage's perceptions of the process were one of a contiguous process. Early mages didn't realize there were such things as &quot;avatars&quot; because the fusion of human and avatar was so perfect it never felt like an external addition to their being once it gain power along with them. For untold times, teachers instructed those with potential until their power awakened, and it seemed as natural as any other craft or endeavor for this to be the case.<br /> <br /> Then things changed. For whatever reasons the avatar-human matching process began to crack. Mages began to be aware of having an avatar as a separate entity because their avatars were no longer indistinguishable from themselves. The Order of Reason had something to sink their teeth into besides being splinter Hermetics; evidence that mages were playing with forces far more malleable than previous generations really thought, and reality itself was the playing field mages were actually fighting on. As the OoR's success in cracking open reality bore fruit in their war with the Traditions, it also meant that the why of who awakens changed as well. The growing mismatch of avatar to person, and the formation of Reality Zones, created the space for more and more unpredictable and seemingly random awakenings. The old studious methods still worked in their way, but it became more and more clear as the Enlightment era kicked in that it wasn't how things worked any more and was just one path among many.<br /> <br /> And change continued. Spontaneous awakening from personal epiphany grew to be the norm, with experienced mages fighting to find and recruit over bolstering their numbers by training. The dichotomy of avatar and mind grew larger and increasingly frayed perceptions of nascent mages to force epiphanies away from the tutelage of others to generate such events. The new generations of mages bucked against the ways of the older generations because the world had literally changed and the rules of reality they had to deal with were not bound by ancient wisdom's teachings. The continuity of mentor to student frayed as it simply became less realistic and few of the older generations were capable of adapting to stay relevant (though a new middle generation born to this new world are beginning to be the new teachers with that test in front of them).<br /> <br /> Which leaves mages where the game starts. Anyone might have the spark of awakening in them. Individuality has taken over from tradition to constantly refuse predictability. Only the must basic fundamental metaphysical truths dictate who can awakening in a near tautological way.<br /> <br /> And of course, stepping back out of the game, this is completely on purpose. The game wants mages to be anybody from every walk of life. And most of what I said is later additions and retcons the game as offered to explain how things got the desire point of the original premise.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Where can I read more about the Avatar relationship with the Psychopomps you described?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T18:22:36-08:00","id":1023524,"title":"","content":"IIRC most of the information on the nature of PSychopomps was detailed in Rogue Council, but the idea was spread throughout the later half of Revised.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T09:11:05-08:00","id":1024368,"title":"","content":"What Heavy Arms said a bit back...<br /> <br /> It is also worth noting that, in many ways, all the Factions and their constituent Sects are playing a game of catch-up since the shift. They would LOVE things to go the old way.... You find likely people, expose them to the wonders of whatever, train them up and, often enough, you get what you were looking for. But the randomness of most Awakenings makes that less than effective (though they certainly all seem set up so they keep doing it, even though it doesn't have a great chance to work). It seems like the set-up is that, for the most part, folks Awaken for whatever reason but mostly <i>on their own</i>. Outside the loving embrace of any Sect. Training them up and seeing if they pop (mages) or just fizzle along (sorcerers), ends up with mostly fizzle. And there is definitely material out there that indicates that once you fizzle, you are less likely to pop later on.<br /> <br /> That means the new reality is that almost all Mages were never Sorcerers, meaning whatever &quot;mystic training&quot; they may have had was just the pale, leaked out, thin shadows of knowledge of true magic (static and dynamic). The kind of stuff you find in the occult section of a bookstore or a &quot;make your own robot&quot; project. Sure, they can start building their Focus with it, but it's more of tacking their newfound power onto some kind of half-assed framework. In essence, they have to start out building their Focus themselves. Most mages start off as quasi-Orphans.<br /> <br /> That's why most Awakenings are like house fires or car accidents . They happen. Somehow they're detected. Everyone who detects it dives in like some kind of competing fire departments and/or ambulance companies and try to snatch up the newbie as quickly as possible. Then they take the fledgling and try to imprint them on THEIR way of doing things. Usually along some lines associated with their first, fumbling attempts to build a Focus (which ties back to their knowledge set, their experiences, their personal proclivities, etc. as per their being quasi-Orphans above). Alternatively, some kind of mage Meyers-Briggs to assess which group would be most suitable. Or they don't and shoehorn the newbie into whatever slot they want them to occupy, which would lead to a lot of pissed off Avatars... a sort of spiritual &quot;File Type Mismatch&quot;.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, this makes the various Factions and Sects more diverse but also more of a hodgepodge of folks. Pretty much everyone is coming at the &quot;whatever&quot; of the group in their own way. And diversity isn't really a strength for &quot;group think&quot;. Which makes the reasons for the groups hangning together as separate groups a lot weaker. Nightshade's Verbena aren't what the Verbena are now. Her table of Old Faith-ers may be a significant fraction of folks in the group, but pretty much anyone within a very vague &quot;type&quot; are under the tent of the current Sect... from New Agers and Wiccans with REAL magic now to some folks trying out Mesoamerican blood rituals to modern ethnobotanists researching the anthropology of folk cures over the biochemistry (though they know that too) and so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 12-30-2016, 12:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}